w and that's why from july  of this year we are increasing the  national insurance thresholds to save a  typical worker 330 pounds a year with 70  percent of employees to be brought off  as a result of those changes that we're  making so those are the things that  there's a tax cuts that are going to be  coming into effect i understand you know  the conservative principle that you know  a marginal pound is is better spent by  an individual than the state i get that  but we are in extraordinary times here  inflation is at nine percent there's a  lot of people on benefits who are  worried about keeping their children  warm about putting food on the table so  actually for them  perhaps that marginal pound spent by the  state is is the difference between  keeping their families warm and not or  or getting an extra loaf of bread at the  weekend right  do they move  as i said at the outset we're very much  aware that many families will be  struggling right now and particularly  for those people on benefits there are a  huge amount of resources available to  them for example if you look at an  average household in the outgoings it's  things like their bills their utility  bills that's why i won't mention it a  third time but help on council tax but  for people on benefits it goes even  further there's help to assist on water  bills there's help to assist um on  paying child care that's not just  available for people on benefits that's  actually available to a very wide number  of people and actually there's many  people thousands of households who are  not actually claiming the 15 or 13 at  the same time though hours free child  care paid for by the government there's  going up by 3.7  universal credit's going up by 3.7  inflation is going up today by 9 i mean  i'm not a mathematician but you know and  i know that things are getting very very  hard for families and actually just  saying actually look there's loads of  help around there people just need to  look a bit harder to find it is that  really  sufficient  listen i i as i say we are very  cognizant of the struggles that many  families are facing however i just want  to say it's not fair to say that the  government has done nothing and that  we're somehow not responding okay we've  announced a 22 billion pound package  that's significant we've announced  significant tax cuts um  when do you think more help as rishi  sunak signaled when will that come will  that come in july before the summer  recess  listen you're asking me something that  really is going to be a decision for the  chancellor and the prime minister and  then the cabinet uh he's made an  announcement today that's going to be  worked through and in terms of uh how  that's announced um but i can't give you  any timelines on announcements but if  the chancellor says there will be tax  cuts coming i trust i trust him and i'm  very glad to hear it okay now you of  course are the attorney general you're  the legal advisor to the government  if the uk government unilaterally scraps  or  ignores parts of the northern ireland  protocol without the agreement of the eu  with of course whom you signed the  brexit deal with would that be breaking  the law  i can't get into the details of legal  advice unfortunately i know that might  sound a bit odd but there's a convention  in government which means that the law  officers can't comment on the fact or  the content of their legal advice but  we do know uh what the foreign secretary  has said and she has made very clear in  her statement yesterday that the action  proposed by the government has a lawful  basis and there will be a statement  which will be issued by the government  in due course setting out a bit more  detail behind that so i'm not able to go  into the details behind the legalities  what i am able to say is that the  measures announced are important vital  and have my support it's really uh  there's there are challenges and  problems in northern ireland caused by  the operation of the protocol uh in  relation to uh i mean and the effects of  those problems are visible for everyone  to see we've seen the collapse of  stormont so that people in northern  ireland are not going to be getting the  services that they deserve because of a  paralysis in their government we've seen  um more uh  burdens and costs uh imposed on traders  based in northern ireland because of  excessive  bureaucracy uh um you know affected by  the eu's application of the rules in the  protocol so there are problems uh we  want to get stormont back up and running  we want to ensure that  northern ireland's place in within the  uh territory of the united kingdom is  secured and reassured and that's why  these proposals are very important  i just want to ask a really sort of  simple uh question and you know you're  saying that there's a convention not to  publish the uh legal advice but at the  same time we are in uh quite an  extraordinary situation uh the situation  of course in northern ireland uh  extremely uh um you know fragile very  lots of people are very very concerned  about what's happening there will you  commit to publishing  the legal advice on this  the foreign secretaries make clear that  we're going to be publishing a statement  so that's a bit different though isn't  it so just can we just unpick that bit  will you publish the legal advice that  you gave to the government or are we  just going to rely on on a statement  what will be in that statement  what's actually happening no we're not  going to publish the legal advice it's  incredibly rare uh for the government to  publish its legal advice i don't think  it's actually uh ever really been done  before so if that there are you know any  lawyer will know uh legal advice is  privileged and confidential that means  even more in the context of government  and uh you know this is an important  issue and uh you know the government has  the right to keep its uh legal advice  private and confidential i i hope you'd  respect that anybody else has that right  but i think that what the government is  doing as i said it will be setting out a  bit more detail about the legal basis so  that people have a bit more of an  understanding about why we think the  action taken is lawful i just want to  try to work out your red lines on what's  happening at the minute with the  protocol because you know talking to  some people on the eu side they say look  we're willing to try and find a landing  zone where there's fewer checks needed  do you want to see the role of the  european court of justice removed in  ruling and disputes in northern ireland  yes  uh the european court of justice should  not be the final arbiter of disputes  between the uk and the eu and the bill  that we have committed to introducing  will reflect that  there's no way there's no way that  you're going to get an agreement with  the eu on this is there frankly if if  that is a red line for you  well i i've i've been talking about  issues relating to the european union  for many for too many years now to know  that uh those in the media who predict  uh the worse aren't often uh proven to  be right sophie with respect so i i  wouldn't want to prejudge the outcome of  what happens with these uh with this  issue i very much hope the eu will uh  respond uh creatively uh positively  constructively the ideal outcome for all  of us is that we get a renegotiated  settlement that works for  the united kingdom and in particular  works for the uh the northern ire the  communities in northern ireland and  upholds the primacy of the good friday  agreement the foundation of peace but  also respects the uh the the boundaries  of the european union single market  which the uk really does pay a lot of  respect to okay and very just just  quickly because i do want to talk to you  about one other topic as well but you  know some people listening to you will  find this frankly quite astonishing  that the uk is willing to unilaterally  effectively rip up major parts of an  agreement that you signed that people  said would lead to  checks along the rsc that boris johnson  sold as a brilliant deal and now you're  just saying that actually no we can just  unilaterally rip the whole thing up  and it's not such a great deal after all  what is going on  i think what most people will find very  astonishing uh  more worryingly actually is how the  situation in northern ireland has been  detrimentally affected by the eu's  application of the protocol we and 54 of  the 19 members of the northern ireland  assembly are in favor of keeping the um  protocol as it is rather than scrapping  it  well well you'll know well actually uh  the majority all parties actually agree  that there are profound problems with  the protocol so that's actually not  controversial and the majority the  largest group on the executive the  unionists all agree that the protocol  must be changed fundamentally so i i  don't think you know i think the people  in northern ireland 54 of the 90 members  of the northern ireland assembly are  from parties who are in favor of not  doing what you want to do with the  protocol but in effectively maintaining  it  well what we know is that in northern  ireland uh because of the eu's  application of the protocol we are  seeing onerous  checks and costs being applied to  traders the the chairman of marx and  spencer's has come out and said that the  way it's being implemented is  is bureaucratic it's making it's very  difficult for uh making all it's almost  making it's making it almost impossible  for business to be done  and that actually the disproportionate  level of checks uh just doesn't really  isn't justifiable and that's what we  want to remedy we want to find a  compromise and we want to definitely  find uh an agreement that works as i say  for northern ireland for all communities  for both communities and for the eu and  for the uk uh now we have seen more  allegations 